    "id": "10.1111/1751-7915.70485",
    "title": "A Droplet PCR‐Based Single‐Cell Screening Method Revealed the Diverse Potential Natural Hosts of PromAγ Plasmids",
    "abstract": "A droplet digital PCR‐based single‐cell method enabled direct detection and recovery of bacteria harbouring PromAγ plasmids from lake sediments. This approach revealed unexpectedly diverse potential natural hosts, including uncultured taxa, suggesting the ecological versatility of broad‐host‐range plasmids.\n\nABSTRACT\nPromA plasmids are a broad‐host‐range plasmid group and have been isolated from various environmental samples by exogenous plasmid capturing methods. However, their natural hosts have remained unidentified. Here, we developed a cultivation‐independent single‐cell‐level screening approach combining water‐in‐oil droplets and digital PCR with a fluorescent probe, which enabled direct detection of PromAγ‐related repA signals associated with environmental bacterial taxa. The positive droplets were collected by a dispenser with microfluidics and the potential natural hosts of the plasmid were identified based on their 16S rRNA gene sequences. Application to Lake Sanaru sediment samples collected on two dates identified diverse bacterial taxa associated with PromAγ‐related repA signals, including previously unreported candidate hosts. Our results suggested that PromAγ plasmids were associated with a wide variety of bacterial taxa in natural microbial communities. The developed methods could be applied for the detection and collection of bacterial cells with a targeted gene, which would be valuable for microbial ecology studies.\n\nA droplet digital PCR-based single-cell method enabled direct detection and recovery of bacteria harbouring PromAγ plasmids from lake sediments. This approach revealed unexpectedly diverse potential natural hosts, including uncultured taxa, suggesting the ecological versatility of broad-host-range plasmids.\n\nABSTRACT\nPromA plasmids are a broad-host-range plasmid group and have been isolated from various environmental samples by exogenous plasmid capturing methods. However, their natural hosts have remained unidentified. Here, we developed a cultivation-independent single-cell-level screening approach combining water-in-oil droplets and digital PCR with a fluorescent probe, which enabled direct detection of PromAγ-related repA signals associated with environmental bacterial taxa. The positive droplets were collected by a dispenser with microfluidics and the potential natural hosts of the plasmid were identified based on their 16S rRNA gene sequences. Application to Lake Sanaru sediment samples collected on two dates identified diverse bacterial taxa associated with PromAγ-related repA signals, including previously unreported candidate hosts. Our results suggested that PromAγ plasmids were associated with a wide variety of bacterial taxa in natural microbial communities. The developed methods could be applied for the detection and collection of bacterial cells with a targeted gene, which would be valuable for microbial ecology studies.",
