    "id": "10.64898/2026.11.23.768638",
    "title": "A cell-based screen identifies host-directed inhibitors and nutrient-conditional antimicrobials against intracellular S. flexneri dissemination",
    "abstract": "Multidrug-resistant S. flexneri is a World Health Organization priority pathogen for which no universally licensed vaccine is currently available. Most antimicrobial screening platforms involve planktonic bacteria replicating in nutrient-rich medium, a condition that does not reflect the intracellular niche in which S. flexneri replicates and spreads and systematically excludes compounds whose activity depends on the host environment. Here, we screened the 1,581 compounds of the National Cancer Institute Diversity Set VII for inhibitors of S. flexneri dissemination in human colonic HT-29 monolayers by high-content microscopy. By applying sequential counter-screens, we identified six hits falling into two mechanistically distinct classes. Four compounds restricted dissemination without detectable antibacterial activity or host toxicity at the concentrations used. NSC310113 and Thiosangivamycin (NSC105827) implicate host matrix metalloprotease activity in cell-to-cell spread and host CDK2 in epithelial cell invasion. Two additional compounds, NSC45641 and NSC367428, were antibacte-rial only under nutrient-limited conditions and would have been described as inactive by conventional screening. NSC45641 is an adenine analog imposing a purine limitation reversible by adenine supplementation. The uracil analog NSC367428, whose activity requires the bacterial uracil phosphoribosyltransferase Upp, is consistent with perturbation of pyrimidine metabolism in both minimal medium and in HT-29 cells. Together, these findings demonstrate that cell-based dissemination screening of the NCI Diversity Set VII identified two classes of inhibitors that would likely be missed by conventional nutrient-rich medium screening, highlighting nutrient-conditional antimicrobials as promising starting points for developing treatments against intracellular pathogens.",
