    "id": "10.64898/2026.11.23.768699",
    "title": "Cold atmospheric plasma for improved sustainability and fish welfare in recirculating aquaculture systems",
    "abstract": "Decontamination of water is mandatory for the cultivation of aquatic species in recirculating aquaculture systems (RAS). Avoiding economic and ecological losses while ensuring safe conditions is important for the future introduction of new technologies. Therefore, the implementation of cold atmospheric plasma (CAP) within RAS was examined for the first time. For the integration of this technology two equal RAS were set up, one using CAP, the other one ultraviolet light (UV) for water decontamination. Experiments with Rainbow trout (Oncorhynchus mykiss) were performed for three weeks to compare CAP with UV as reference. The CAP device was switched on intermittently for 8 hours daily (with intervals of 10 min on and 30 min off), while two UV lamps were turned on continuously throughout the entire time of 3 weeks, which required more energy. Fish development was tracked by fish weight and stress levels (cortisol measurements). Values for pH, conductivity, oxygen, temperature, microbial load, microbiome and ion composition were also monitored. Microbial loads for both setups were reduced by four orders of magnitude. The biodiversity of the microbiome of the two technologies was similar with a beta-diversity between 19 and 25. Deviations within each setup were low with the alpha-diversity ranging from 2.23-2.93 (CAP) and 2.42-2.79 (UV). The water monitoring revealed similar results for pH value, conductivity, oxygen and temperature. No differences for the cortisol measurements and also the fish weight were determined. The results demonstrate CAP as a competitive and more energy efficient alternative to UV benefiting fish welfare and development in a similar way. The equal development of fish and water parameters and low energy consumption compared to UV makes CAP an energy efficient tool, likewise enhancing fish welfare.",
