    "id": "10.1093/bioadv/vbag327",
    "title": "A multiscale framework for hypothesis generation from sex-stratified X-linked gene networks.",
    "abstract": "MotivationStratified gene co-expression network analyses compare molecular organization across biological groups; however, their interpretation is challenged by cohort imbalance, network instability, correlation-method dependence, and multiplicity. These issues are particularly relevant when comparing network structures across heterogeneous cohorts, where apparent patterns may not be reproducible.ResultsThis study presents a reproducible framework for stratified gene co-expression network analysis. It integrates cohort balancing, bootstrap-stabilised consensus networks, topology-based statistics, paired label permutations, multiplicity control, and correlation-method benchmarking. Its design distinguishes robust statistical evidence from exploratory signals and their biological interpretation. The framework is illustrated through a multi-cancer case study of sex-associated co-expression network organization among X-linked genes classified as escape, inactive, or variably escaping from X-chromosome inactivation. The analysis included 15 cancer cohorts from The Cancer Genome Atlas (TCGA) and 90 sex-stratified networks, enabling systematic comparison of network structure across cancer types and X-inactivation gene classes. Across 315 paired permutation tests, none survived Benjamini-Hochberg correction, providing no multiplicity-adjusted evidence. Nevertheless, exploratory graphlet profiles revealed cancer- and gene-class-specific patterns of network organization, highlighting candidate structures for independent replication. The case study illustrates how the framework can distinguish nominal or visually compelling differences from multiplicity-adjusted evidence while preserving informative exploratory patterns for structured hypothesis generation.Availability and implementationThe pipeline source code is available at https://github.com/ad03-coder/xgenes. The analysis uses data generated by the TCGA Research Network (https://www.cancer.gov/tcga), obtained from the GDC Data Portal (https://portal.gdc.cancer.gov/).",
