    "id": "10.1111/1462-2920.70511",
    "title": "Mechanistic Transition From Phosphate Adsorption to Precipitation on Dolomite Under Calco‐Carbonic Equilibrium: Implications for Microbial Phosphorus Cycling",
    "abstract": "Dolomite‐mediated phosphate retention involves adsorption and precipitation, controlling carbonate effects on P availability and microbial nutrient acquisition. Distinguishing their controlling conditions is therefore essential for understanding phosphorus immobilization and its implications for microbial nutrient cycling. Phosphate immobilization by dolomite is governed by interacting concentration, reaction time and temperature and provides mechanistic insights into phosphate bioavailability.\n\nABSTRACT\nDolomite can remove phosphate (PO43−) through surface adsorption and mineral precipitation, two mechanisms that differ in phosphorus retention, stability and bioavailability. Distinguishing their controlling conditions is therefore essential for understanding phosphorus immobilization and its implications for microbial nutrient cycling. This study investigates the critical conditions governing the transition between these two processes under calco‐carbonic equilibrium by adjusting PO43− concentration, reaction time and temperature. Kinetic experiment results showed a clear concentration–time and temperature‐dependent shift in the dominant removal mechanism. At 25°C and 35°C, adsorption predominated at initial phosphate concentrations \nDolomite-mediated phosphate retention involves adsorption and precipitation, controlling carbonate effects on P availability and microbial nutrient acquisition. Distinguishing their controlling conditions is therefore essential for understanding phosphorus immobilization and its implications for microbial nutrient cycling. Phosphate immobilization by dolomite is governed by interacting concentration, reaction time and temperature and provides mechanistic insights into phosphate bioavailability.\n\nABSTRACT\nDolomite can remove phosphate (PO4\n3−) through surface adsorption and mineral precipitation, two mechanisms that differ in phosphorus retention, stability and bioavailability. Distinguishing their controlling conditions is therefore essential for understanding phosphorus immobilization and its implications for microbial nutrient cycling. This study investigates the critical conditions governing the transition between these two processes under calco-carbonic equilibrium by adjusting PO4\n3− concentration, reaction time and temperature. Kinetic experiment results showed a clear concentration–time and temperature-dependent shift in the dominant removal mechanism. At 25°C and 35°C, adsorption predominated at initial phosphate concentrations < 10 mg L−1 within 48 h, whereas precipitation became dominant at concentrations ≥ 20 mg L−1 after 24 h. At 15°C, adsorption remained predominant below 30 mg L−1 for 72 h, while precipitation emerged at concentrations ≥ 50 mg L−1 after 48 h. IR and XPS spectroscopy indicated progressive phosphate association with the dolomite surface under precipitation-favourable conditions. SEM provided evidence of phosphate-bearing precipitate formation. Removal kinetics and Freundlich model further identified concentration- and time-dependent transition thresholds. These findings demonstrate that phosphate immobilization by dolomite is governed by interacting concentration, reaction time and temperature, and provide mechanistic insights into phosphate retention and stability and informing dolomite-based remediation strategies.",
