    "title": "Targeting Quorum Sensing With SD8: An Anti‐Virulence Strategy That Attenuates Pseudomonas aeruginosa Pathogenesis and Potentiates Antibiotics",
    "abstract": "This work demonstrated that SD8 showed potential as a novel anti‐virulence agent against P. aeruginosa. SD8 inhibited multiple virulence‐related phenotypes and downregulated QS‐related gene expression. Notably, combination assays demonstrated that SD8 enhanced the antibacterial activity of several antibiotics and significantly increased the survival rate of the host in the infection models.\n\nABSTRACT\nPseudomonas aeruginosa exhibits complex tolerance and adaptive capabilities, enabling it to persist under antibiotic pressure during infection and thereby complicating treatment. In recent years, strategies targeting virulence rather than bacterial growth have gained increasing attention. Among them, the quorum sensing (QS) system, which integrates multiple pathogenic processes, has been recognized as an important potential target. This study aimed to investigate the anti‐virulence activity of 7‐((2‐methylquinolin‐6‐yl)methyl)‐7H‐pyrrolo[3,2‐f]quinazoline‐1,3‐diamine (SD8) against P. aeruginosa. SD8 showed no detectable antibacterial activity under standard MIC conditions and had little effect on bacterial growth in nutrient‐rich medium, although growth inhibition was observed in M9‐adenosine medium. SD8 inhibited multiple virulence‐related phenotypes of P. aeruginosa, including biofilm formation, pyocyanin production, protease activity, rhamnolipid production and motility. Quantitative real‐time PCR (qPCR) analysis further showed that SD8 reduced the expression of several QS‐related genes. Molecular docking suggested that SD8 may interact with the ligand‐binding pockets of QS receptors. Moreover, combination assays showed that SD8 enhanced the antibacterial activity of polymyxin B and tobramycin. SD8 treatment also significantly improved the survival of infected C. elegans and mice. Overall, SD8 exerts anti‐virulence effects by interfering with QS‐regulated pathways and represents a potential candidate for anti‐virulence therapy.\n\nThis work demonstrated that SD8 showed potential as a novel anti-virulence agent against P. aeruginosa. SD8 inhibited multiple virulence-related phenotypes and downregulated QS-related gene expression. Notably, combination assays demonstrated that SD8 enhanced the antibacterial activity of several antibiotics and significantly increased the survival rate of the host in the infection models.\n\nABSTRACT\nPseudomonas aeruginosa exhibits complex tolerance and adaptive capabilities, enabling it to persist under antibiotic pressure during infection and thereby complicating treatment. In recent years, strategies targeting virulence rather than bacterial growth have gained increasing attention. Among them, the quorum sensing (QS) system, which integrates multiple pathogenic processes, has been recognized as an important potential target. This study aimed to investigate the anti-virulence activity of 7-((2-methylquinolin-6-yl)methyl)-7H-pyrrolo[3,2-f]quinazoline-1,3-diamine (SD8) against P. aeruginosa. SD8 showed no detectable antibacterial activity under standard MIC conditions and had little effect on bacterial growth in nutrient-rich medium, although growth inhibition was observed in M9-adenosine medium. SD8 inhibited multiple virulence-related phenotypes of P. aeruginosa, including biofilm formation, pyocyanin production, protease activity, rhamnolipid production and motility. Quantitative real-time PCR (qPCR) analysis further showed that SD8 reduced the expression of several QS-related genes. Molecular docking suggested that SD8 may interact with the ligand-binding pockets of QS receptors. Moreover, combination assays showed that SD8 enhanced the antibacterial activity of polymyxin B and tobramycin. SD8 treatment also significantly improved the survival of infected C. elegans and mice. Overall, SD8 exerts anti-virulence effects by interfering with QS-regulated pathways and represents a potential candidate for anti-virulence therapy.",
