    "id": "10.1111/1751-7915.70495",
    "title": "An Excess‐Sludge‐Derived Enterococcus faecium With Low‐Molecular‐Weight Organic Acid‐Producing Potential: Functional Evaluation and Metabolic Characterisation",
    "abstract": "Enterococcus faecium WN‐18, isolated from excess sludge, converted glucose into comparable amounts of low‐molecular‐weight organic acids under aerobic and anaerobic conditions. Oxygen availability redirected pyruvate‐derived metabolism, favoring acetate and propionate aerobically and lactate and formate anaerobically, thereby providing a practical means to tailor the acid composition.\n\nABSTRACT\nLow‐molecular‐weight organic acids (LMWOAs) are widely applied as carbon sources and electron donors in environmental biotechnology; however, their external supplementation increases operational costs. In this study, the LMWOA‐producing strain WN‐18 was isolated from excess sludge and identified as Enterococcus faecium. WN‐18 produced lactic acid, acetic acid, propionic acid and formic acid under both aerobic and anaerobic conditions. After 28 h, total net LMWOA accumulation reached 17.36 ± 1.24 and 18.13 ± 0.59 g/L under aerobic and anaerobic conditions, respectively (mean ± SD, n = 3). The corresponding apparent total LMWOA yields were 0.87 ± 0.06 and 0.91 ± 0.03 g/g measured glucose depletion, respectively. Acetic and propionic acid accumulation was higher under aerobic cultivation, whereas lactic acid accumulation was higher under anaerobic cultivation. Genome annotation revealed a complete glycolytic pathway and genes associated with lactate, formate and acetate production. Among the nine Enterococcus genomes examined, glycolysis and the major gene groups related to formate and acetate production were conserved, whereas sugar uptake and putative propionate‐associated components varied among strains. At 10 h, anaerobic cultures showed higher expression of ldhA and pflD and lower expression of pta, pdhB and aceF than in aerobic cultures. These expression patterns corresponded to greater mean lactate and formate accumulation and lower acetate accumulation in anaerobic cultures at 28 h. WN‐18 produced an acetate‐ and propionate‐enriched fermentate under aerobic cultivation and a lactate‐rich fermentate under anaerobic cultivation, a distinction that highlights the potential of this sludge‐derived strain for bioprocesses with different organic acid requirements.\n\nEnterococcus faecium WN-18, isolated from excess sludge, converted glucose into comparable amounts of low-molecular-weight organic acids under aerobic and anaerobic conditions. Oxygen availability redirected pyruvate-derived metabolism, favoring acetate and propionate aerobically and lactate and formate anaerobically, thereby providing a practical means to tailor the acid composition.\n\nABSTRACT\nLow-molecular-weight organic acids (LMWOAs) are widely applied as carbon sources and electron donors in environmental biotechnology; however, their external supplementation increases operational costs. In this study, the LMWOA-producing strain WN-18 was isolated from excess sludge and identified as Enterococcus faecium. WN-18 produced lactic acid, acetic acid, propionic acid and formic acid under both aerobic and anaerobic conditions. After 28 h, total net LMWOA accumulation reached 17.36 ± 1.24 and 18.13 ± 0.59 g/L under aerobic and anaerobic conditions, respectively (mean ± SD, n = 3). The corresponding apparent total LMWOA yields were 0.87 ± 0.06 and 0.91 ± 0.03 g/g measured glucose depletion, respectively. Acetic and propionic acid accumulation was higher under aerobic cultivation, whereas lactic acid accumulation was higher under anaerobic cultivation. Genome annotation revealed a complete glycolytic pathway and genes associated with lactate, formate and acetate production. Among the nine Enterococcus genomes examined, glycolysis and the major gene groups related to formate and acetate production were conserved, whereas sugar uptake and putative propionate-associated components varied among strains. At 10 h, anaerobic cultures showed higher expression of ldhA and pflD and lower expression of pta, pdhB and aceF than in aerobic cultures. These expression patterns corresponded to greater mean lactate and formate accumulation and lower acetate accumulation in anaerobic cultures at 28 h. WN-18 produced an acetate- and propionate-enriched fermentate under aerobic cultivation and a lactate-rich fermentate under anaerobic cultivation, a distinction that highlights the potential of this sludge-derived strain for bioprocesses with different organic acid requirements.",
