    "id": "10.1111/mec.70664",
    "title": "Long‐Term Effects of Clear‐Cutting on Fungal Communities in Boreal Forest Soils",
    "abstract": "ABSTRACT\nClear‐cut forestry has transformed boreal forests in Fennoscandia into a mosaic of homogeneous forest stands in different successional stages, while the proportion of old‐growth forests that have not been clear‐cut declines every year. We investigated long‐term consequences of clear‐cut forestry on fungal communities in boreal forest soils, focusing on how fungal communities in different soil depths respond to forestry. We established a paired‐plot study design that included 12 Norway spruce stands, clear‐cut 50–90 years ago and now ready for another round of clear‐cutting, each paired with a nearby near‐natural, non‐clear‐cut spruce forest. Fungal communities in seven different soil horizons, including two organic and five mineral horizons, were analysed using DNA metabarcoding of the ITS2 region as a fungal marker. While richness did not differ between the two forest types, community composition did. The fungal communities were strongly structured by soil depth, and a difference in community composition was found between the two forest types in most soil horizons. Numerous fungal groups showed strong long‐term responses to clear‐cut forestry. Archaeorhizomycetes, Elaphomycetaceae and Leotiales were more abundant in near‐natural forest soils and showed clear preferences for different soil horizons. Among ectomycorrhizal fungi, Cortinarius spp. were strongly associated with near‐natural forests. Our results demonstrate that clear‐cut forestry has long‐term effects on boreal soil fungal communities decades after clear‐cutting took place, even in deeper mineral horizons. Responses differed among genera and within ecological guilds, emphasising the importance of investigating the responses within guilds and not solely overall fungal community patterns.\nABSTRACT\nClear-cut forestry has transformed boreal forests in Fennoscandia into a mosaic of homogeneous forest stands in different successional stages, while the proportion of old-growth forests that have not been clear-cut declines every year. We investigated long-term consequences of clear-cut forestry on fungal communities in boreal forest soils, focusing on how fungal communities in different soil depths respond to forestry. We established a paired-plot study design that included 12 Norway spruce stands, clear-cut 50–90 years ago and now ready for another round of clear-cutting, each paired with a nearby near-natural, non-clear-cut spruce forest. Fungal communities in seven different soil horizons, including two organic and five mineral horizons, were analysed using DNA metabarcoding of the ITS2 region as a fungal marker. While richness did not differ between the two forest types, community composition did. The fungal communities were strongly structured by soil depth, and a difference in community composition was found between the two forest types in most soil horizons. Numerous fungal groups showed strong long-term responses to clear-cut forestry. Archaeorhizomycetes, Elaphomycetaceae and Leotiales were more abundant in near-natural forest soils and showed clear preferences for different soil horizons. Among ectomycorrhizal fungi, Cortinarius spp. were strongly associated with near-natural forests. Our results demonstrate that clear-cut forestry has long-term effects on boreal soil fungal communities decades after clear-cutting took place, even in deeper mineral horizons. Responses differed among genera and within ecological guilds, emphasising the importance of investigating the responses within guilds and not solely overall fungal community patterns.",
