    "id": "10.1111/mec.70673",
    "title": "Geoclimatic and Host‐Driven Speciation in Colletotrichum",
    "abstract": "ABSTRACT\nUnderstanding speciation in plant pathogens is essential for predicting the emergence of novel virulent strains and deciphering biodiversity dynamics. This study explored the evolutionary history and diversification patterns of Colletotrichum, a globally significant plant‐pathogenic fungal genus. Divergence dating analysis places the origin of Colletotrichum in the middle Eocene (~47.57 Mya) with an ancestral state of broad host range, followed by host specialization particularly towards monocots and legumes during the Eocene–Oligocene transition (~32–39 Mya), a period coinciding with global cooling and aridification. While early‐diverging lineages formed during this period (i.e., C. gigasporum and C. orbiculare species complexes) exhibit limited extant diversity, the genus in general underwent accelerated diversification from the late Miocene onward. This acceleration is particularly prominent within the C. gloeosporioides species complex, which may be related to Pliocene vegetation shifts and late Neogene‐Quaternary environmental changes. Genomic analyses revealed that genome size evolution in Colletotrichum is significantly correlated with phylogenetic constraints and repetitive sequence ratio. We also observed extensive gene tree‐species tree incongruence, suggesting roles for incomplete lineage sorting or introgression. Overall, our findings reveal a macroevolutionary pattern in which ancient climatic shifts, host adaptation dynamics, and genomic plasticity have jointly shaped Colletotrichum diversification, while possible anthropogenic effects on the youngest lineages remain further investigation.\nABSTRACT\nUnderstanding speciation in plant pathogens is essential for predicting the emergence of novel virulent strains and deciphering biodiversity dynamics. This study explored the evolutionary history and diversification patterns of Colletotrichum, a globally significant plant-pathogenic fungal genus. Divergence dating analysis places the origin of Colletotrichum in the middle Eocene (~47.57 Mya) with an ancestral state of broad host range, followed by host specialization particularly towards monocots and legumes during the Eocene–Oligocene transition (~32–39 Mya), a period coinciding with global cooling and aridification. While early-diverging lineages formed during this period (i.e., C. gigasporum and C. orbiculare species complexes) exhibit limited extant diversity, the genus in general underwent accelerated diversification from the late Miocene onward. This acceleration is particularly prominent within the C. gloeosporioides species complex, which may be related to Pliocene vegetation shifts and late Neogene-Quaternary environmental changes. Genomic analyses revealed that genome size evolution in Colletotrichum is significantly correlated with phylogenetic constraints and repetitive sequence ratio. We also observed extensive gene tree-species tree incongruence, suggesting roles for incomplete lineage sorting or introgression. Overall, our findings reveal a macroevolutionary pattern in which ancient climatic shifts, host adaptation dynamics, and genomic plasticity have jointly shaped Colletotrichum diversification, while possible anthropogenic effects on the youngest lineages remain further investigation.",
