    "id": "10.1186/s40168-026-02586-4",
    "title": "Short-term antibiotic treatment alters human blood neutrophil activation and gene regulation",
    "abstract": "Background\n                     The gut microbiota provides key signals that shape neutrophil development and function. Although antibiotic-induced disruption of the microbiota has been shown to affect innate immune responses in experimental systems, evidence defining how acute microbiota perturbation influences human neutrophil biology under physiological conditions remains limited.\n                  \n                     Results\n                     Here, we investigated the effects of a short course of broad-spectrum antibiotics on gut microbiota composition and neutrophil phenotype and regulation in healthy adults. Antibiotic exposure resulted in marked microbiota disruption accompanied by reduced short-chain fatty acid production. Neutrophils collected from blood after treatment exhibited increased basal and stimulus-induced cytokine release, and presented a transcriptional profile associated with inflammatory activation and migration. Chromatin accessibility analysis revealed coordinated remodeling after antibiotic exposure, characterized by contraction of promoter accessibility and selective permissiveness at inflammatory regulatory elements. Serum collected after antibiotic treatment was sufficient to increase the secretion of pro-inflammatory cytokines by cells from untreated donors, indicating the involvement of systemic mediators. Notably, supplementation of post-antibiotic serum with short-chain fatty acids partially reversed these effects, supporting a role for microbiota-derived metabolites in restraining inflammatory responses.\n                  \n                     Conclusions\n                     Our results show that short-term antibiotic-induced dysbiosis in humans reshapes blood-derived neutrophil regulatory programs, shifting these cells toward a primed inflammatory state characterized by altered transcriptional and chromatin landscapes.\n                     \nVideo Abstract",
