    "id": "10.3389/fmicb.2026.2027955",
    "title": "Effects of different prebiotics in patients with inflammatory bowel disease: an in vitro fermentation model",
    "abstract": "BackgroundInflammatory bowel disease (IBD) is a chronic and recurrent disorder where gut microbiota modulation plays a critical therapeutic role. Therefore, the aim of this study was to apply an in vitro fermentation model to compare the effects of different prebiotics on samples from IBD patients, with a particular focus on the differences between the active disease phase and the remission phase.MethodsFecal samples from recruited IBD patients were subjected to an in vitro fermentation model using media supplemented with five different prebiotics: lactulose (LAU), fructooligosaccharides (FOS), isomaltooligosaccharides (IMO), mannan oligosaccharides (MOS), and xylooligosaccharides (XOS). Changes in Lactobacillus and Bifidobacterium abundances, short-chain fatty acids (SCFAs), and gas production were evaluated before and after fermentation.ResultsLactulose promoted gas production in remission-stage IBD patients. Treatment with FOS enhanced Lactobacillus and Bifidobacterium proliferation in remission IBD. Mannan oligosaccharides (MOS) predominantly promoted Lactobacillus in active IBD, while increasing Bifidobacterium and gas production in remission IBD. In addition, isomaltooligosaccharides (IMO) increased acetic acid production by elevating Bifidobacterium abundance, particularly in remission. Furthermore, xylooligosaccharides (XOS) increased Bifidobacterium abundances in both active and remission phase of IBD.ConclusionTreatment with dietary prebiotics (including FOS, IMO, MOS, and XOS) improves the Lactobacillus and Bifidobacterium abundances and SCFAs in patients with remission-stage IBD, with MOS and XOS additionally demonstrating favorable impacts during the active phase. These findings highlight the therapeutic potential of specific prebiotics, particularly XOS, in dietary and clinical management strategies for IBD.",
